+# Stock reservations — specification
+
+## Why
+
+Checkout needs to hold stock for a cart while payment runs, so that the
+units it showed the customer are still there when payment succeeds. After
+payment, checkout either turns the hold into an order or gives the stock back.
+Carts are abandoned, and nobody may be around to clean up after them. Holds
+must therefore lapse on their own. The cart service retries on timeouts, and a
+retried request must never hold stock twice.
+
+## Interface (fixed)
+
+`InventoryService(repository, ledger, clock)` keeps working as is.
+
+`inventory.service.InventoryService`:
+
+- `InventoryService.reserve(warehouse: str, items: dict[str, int], idempotency_key: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.confirm_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Order`
+- `InventoryService.release_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.get_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.available(sku, warehouse)` must take holds into account.
+
+`inventory.models`:
+
+- dataclass `Reservation` with `reservation_id: str`, `warehouse: str`,
+  `items: dict[str, int]` (units held per SKU), `status: ReservationStatus`,
+  `expires_at: datetime`, `order_id: str | None`
+- enum `ReservationStatus` with members `ACTIVE`, `CONFIRMED`, `RELEASED`, `EXPIRED`
+
+`inventory.errors`: new `ReservationNotFound`, `ReservationExpired`,
+`InvalidReservationState`, `IdempotencyConflict` (all subclass
+`InventoryError`). Existing errors are reused where they fit (`UnknownWarehouse`,
+`UnknownSku`, `InvalidQuantity`, `InsufficientStock`).
+
+Time comes only from the injected clock. There is no background job: expiry
+must take effect without anyone calling a sweep.
+
+## Terms
+
+- **Hold**: units of a SKU in a warehouse that belong to an ACTIVE
+  reservation.
+- **Lapsed**: an ACTIVE reservation whose `expires_at` is at or before the
+  clock's current time. A lapsed reservation is EXPIRED from then on, and it
+  holds nothing, whether or not anyone has looked at it.
+- **Requested items**: the `items` argument passed to `reserve`. This can
+  differ from `Reservation.items`, which records the units actually held.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+### Holding time
+
+1. A new reservation has `expires_at` = clock time at `reserve` + 20 minutes.
+   The 20-minute default may be made configurable through an optional
+   constructor argument. `InventoryService(repository, ledger, clock)` still
+   gives 20 minutes.
+2. At `now >= expires_at`, an ACTIVE reservation is EXPIRED. That includes
+   `now == expires_at` exactly.
+3. Expiry needs no sweep or other call. Once the clock reaches `expires_at`,
+   the following all reflect it immediately:
+   - `get_reservation` reports `EXPIRED`;
+   - `available` no longer counts the hold;
+   - `reserve` and `place_order` can take those units.
+4. A hold cannot be extended or renewed, and nothing moves `expires_at`.
+5. Only ACTIVE reservations expire. CONFIRMED and RELEASED never change status
+   because of time.
+
+### Reserve
+
+6. `reserve` validates like `place_order`:
+   - unknown warehouse raises `UnknownWarehouse`;
+   - any unknown SKU raises `UnknownSku`;
+   - any quantity that is zero, negative, or not an int (bool included) raises
+     `InvalidQuantity`.
+
+   A rejected request creates nothing and holds nothing, not even for its valid
+   lines.
+7. Empty `items`, or an empty or blank `idempotency_key`, raises some
+   `InventoryError` and creates nothing.
+8. Holds are partial, line by line. Each requested SKU holds
+   `min(requested, available now)` units.
+9. A requested SKU with nothing available is left out of `Reservation.items`.
+   It is not present with 0.
+10. If no requested line can hold anything, `reserve` raises `InsufficientStock`
+    and creates nothing.
+11. On success, the reservation is `ACTIVE`, has a fresh unique
+    `reservation_id`, has `order_id` None, has the requested `warehouse`, and
+    its `items` are exactly the units held.
+12. Holding never writes to the ledger and never changes `on_hand`.
+
+### Idempotency
+
+13. Idempotency keys are global, across all warehouses.
+14. Suppose `reserve` is called with a key already used by an earlier
+    successful `reserve`, with the same warehouse and the same requested items
+    (dict equality). It returns that existing reservation in its current state,
+    whatever the status (ACTIVE, CONFIRMED, RELEASED or EXPIRED):
+    - its `reservation_id` is the same;
+    - no new hold is made and stock is not re-checked;
+    - `expires_at` does not move;
+    - `available` is unchanged by the call.
+15. A key already used with a different warehouse or different requested items
+    raises `IdempotencyConflict`. This applies even when the original
+    reservation is CONFIRMED, RELEASED or EXPIRED. Nothing is created or
+    changed.
+16. A `reserve` call that raised leaves no trace. Its key is still unused, and
+    a later call with that key behaves as a first call.
+
+### Available stock
+
+17. `available(sku, warehouse)` is on-hand minus units allocated to PLACED
+    orders minus units held by ACTIVE, unexpired reservations, for that SKU in
+    that warehouse, floored at 0.
+18. A hold affects only its own warehouse.
+19. `place_order` can only take available stock, so holds reduce what it can
+    take. It remains all-or-nothing.
+20. `receive` and `adjust` are never blocked by holds. If a write-off leaves
+    on-hand below what is held, `available` shows 0 and the existing
+    reservations are left exactly as they are.
+21. `on_hand` and the ledger are never affected by reserve, release, expiry or
+    confirm.
+22. `allocated(sku, warehouse)` keeps its current meaning: units in PLACED
+    orders only. Held units are not included.
+
+### Confirm
+
+23. Confirming an ACTIVE, unexpired reservation places an ordinary `PLACED`
+    order:
+    - the order is in the reservation's warehouse;
+    - it has exactly one line per SKU in `Reservation.items`, with the held
+      quantity (after a partial hold, only what was held);
+    - it gets a normal order id from the existing sequence;
+    - `placed_at` is the clock time at confirm.
+24. After confirm, the reservation is `CONFIRMED` and its `order_id` is the new
+    order's id. `confirm_reservation` returns that order.
+25. Confirm does not change `available` for any SKU, because the held units
+    become allocated units. `on_hand` moves only when the order ships, through
+    the existing `ship_order`.
+26. Confirm succeeds even if a write-off has since left on-hand below holds
+    plus allocations. It does not re-check stock.
+27. Confirming a lapsed reservation raises `ReservationExpired`, including at
+    exactly `expires_at`. No order is created, and the reservation is EXPIRED.
+28. Confirming a CONFIRMED reservation returns the same order (same
+    `order_id`) and creates nothing. This works at any time, including after
+    `expires_at`.
+29. Confirming a RELEASED reservation raises `InvalidReservationState`.
+
+### Release
+
+30. Releasing an ACTIVE, unexpired reservation makes it `RELEASED` and frees
+    its held units immediately. The call returns the reservation.
+31. Releasing a RELEASED reservation is a no-op. It returns the reservation
+    unchanged and raises no error.
+32. Releasing an EXPIRED reservation (including one that has only just lapsed)
+    is a no-op. It returns the reservation unchanged, still `EXPIRED`, and
+    raises no error.
+33. Releasing a CONFIRMED reservation raises `InvalidReservationState`. To undo
+    a confirmed reservation, cancel its order.
+
+### Lookup
+
+34. `get_reservation` returns the reservation with its current status, with
+    lapse taken into account (see 3).
+35. An unknown `reservation_id` raises `ReservationNotFound` from
+    `get_reservation`, `confirm_reservation` and `release_reservation`.
+36. A finished reservation (CONFIRMED, RELEASED or EXPIRED) keeps its `items`
+    and `expires_at` as they were.
+
+### Compatibility
+
+37. The existing test suite passes unchanged.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Extending or renewing a hold, or changing a reservation's items after it is
+  created.
+- Background expiry jobs or an explicit sweep API.
+- Expiring idempotency keys, or scoping them per warehouse or per cart.
+- Reserving across several warehouses in one call.
+- Concurrency or thread safety beyond what the package already offers. It is
+  in-memory and single-threaded.
+- Persistence beyond the existing in-memory repository.
+- Changing the shape of `stock_report` / `StockRow`. Its `available` column
+  reflects holds because it calls `available()`.
+- Any particular reservation id format. Ids only need to be unique.
+- Recording holds or releases in the ledger. They are not stock movements.
